[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router4’s uplink interfaces to the core are down (Layer1/3). Specifically, router4 eth4 (192.168.15.2/24 toward router5) and eth5 (172.25.1.1/24 toward router3) have no carrier.

Mechanism: With both uplinks down, router4 (gateway for Location C 192.168.200.0/24) has no RIP neighbors and no routes to Location A (172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24). Traffic from PC11–14 to 172.16.x is handed to router4, which immediately returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable,” isolating Location C while other zones continue to route via the remaining backbone.

Localization evidence:
- From pc11 to 172.16.10.4: “From 192.168.200.1 Destination Net Unreachable” (router4 generating the reject).
- Router4 routing table shows only C 192.168.200.0/24; no learned routes to 172.16.x.
- Router4 interface state: eth4 and eth5 are DOWN (ip addr shows state DOWN), and ethtool reports “Link detected: no” on both.
- Peers are up: router5 eth5=192.168.15.1/24 UP and router3 eth5=172.25.1.2/24 UP, and they still have routes to 192.168.200.0/24 via router4’s addresses (now black-holed due to link down).

Root cause: Router4’s uplink interfaces eth4 and eth5 are administratively down or physically disconnected, breaking Location C’s path to the rest of the network.

Corrective fix: Restore L1/L2 on router4’s uplinks and rejoin routing. Bring up eth4 and eth5 (e.g., ip link set eth4 up; ip link set eth5 up), verify “Link detected: yes” with ethtool, confirm RIP adjacencies form, and ensure routes to 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24 reappear. After that, PC11–14 should reach Location A normally.
=== END ===